My main interests revolve around the mathematical aspects of (quantum and classical) computation. 
Some broad areas in which I am interested include:
- Quantum Information and Algorithms;
- Hidden Quantum Markov Models and Quantum Markov Chains;
- Theoretical Computer Science, including (classical and quantum) complexity theory, and formal verification;

102693.research:
My current research usually revolves around the topics listed below. This list is by no means meant to be exhaustive, but it provides a nice overview of what I am currently working on with my UCL teammates:



Proof-theoretic semantics, in which I investigate mathematical and philosophical aspects of formal semantics in which the ontological concept of truth is substituted by epistemic concepts such as proof in its role as the fundamental semantic value;



Proof theory and model theory of modal logics in general and intuitionistic modal logics in particular, in which I develop novel proof systems and semantical frameworks for logics with modalities and study how they fare in constructive settings;



Linear Logic, both in its classical and intuitionistic variants, in which I develop novel semantics for classical and intuitionistic linear logic and novel proof systems for the latter;



Logical Ecumenism, in which I study both general conditions under which rival logics may be allowed to peacefully cohexist and technical differences between different approaches to ecumenism;



Logical bilateralism, in which I develop novel proof systems and semantical frameworks for logics with coordinated but independent concepts of proof and refutation.
